1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
In 2023, a single phishing email

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:04,200
drained $4.4 million

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,000
from one crypto wallet.

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
The victim was not a beginner.

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,200
They ran a DeFi protocol.

6
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,500
And they still clicked the wrong link.

7
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Today: how crypto gets stolen —

8
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:17,200
and exactly how to stop it.

9
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:20,000
Not your keys, not your coins.

10
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
This is not a slogan.

11
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
It is a technical fact.

12
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
When you hold crypto on an exchange,

13
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
you do not hold crypto.

14
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,500
You hold a promise from the exchange.

15
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:34,000
The exchange holds the private key.

16
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
If the exchange fails — FTX did —

17
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,500
your promise becomes worthless.

18
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:42,000
A private key is a 256-bit number.

19
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Master password for your funds.

20
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Whoever holds the private key

21
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
controls the funds. Full stop.

22
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Self-custody means you hold your own key.

23
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,500
No exchange. No third party.

24
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:57,500
No counterparty risk.

25
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The tradeoff: entirely your responsibility.

26
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Lose the key — it is gone.

27
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,500
There is no support ticket.

28
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,000
A hot wallet is software

29
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,000
connected to the internet.

30
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
MetaMask. Phantom. Trust Wallet.

31
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,500
Convenient for daily transactions.

32
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:17,000
Exposed to online threats at all times.

33
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Cold wallet: private key always offline.

34
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Ledger. Trezor. Coldcard.

35
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,000
Never connected to the internet

36
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
unless you are actively signing.

37
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The private key never leaves the device.

38
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Your hot wallet is your physical wallet —

39
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
what you carry for coffee money.

40
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Cold wallet = a safe deposit box —

41
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,500
where you keep the serious funds.

42
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:43,000
The device itself is not the asset.

43
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
The seed phrase is the asset.

44
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,500
Seed phrase: 12 or 24 words.

45
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:52,500
Generated once at wallet setup.

46
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,500
It is a human-readable encoding

47
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:57,500
of your private key.

48
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:00,000
If your hardware wallet breaks —

49
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,500
you restore from the seed phrase.

50
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:05,500
If someone finds your seed phrase —

51
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:08,000
they own your funds. Permanently.

52
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,500
The seed phrase is the most important

53
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:13,000
piece of data you will generate.

54
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,500
Not in a photo on your phone.

55
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:18,000
Not in a note-taking app.

56
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Not in a cloud document.

57
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Not emailed to yourself.

58
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Gold standard: a stamped metal backup.

59
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,500
Metal does not burn.

60
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:29,500
Metal does not flood.

61
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:31,500
Metal does not corrupt.

62
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:34,500
Write your seed phrase on metal.

63
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:37,000
Store it somewhere physically secure.

64
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Tell no one.

65
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
One copy = single point of failure.

66
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Two copies in separate locations

67
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,500
is the minimum standard.

68
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:51,000
Here are the five most common ways

69
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,500
crypto gets stolen.

70
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:56,000
Attack one: phishing links.

71
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
A fake website — looks like your wallet.

72
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
You enter your seed phrase.

73
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
It is captured instantly.

74
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Rule: never type your seed phrase online

75
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,500
into a website. Ever.

76
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,500
Attack two: malicious wallet extensions.

77
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:15,500
A browser extension that mimics MetaMask.

78
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,500
It intercepts your transactions.

79
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:21,500
Always download from the official source only.

80
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,500
Check the URL. Check the developer.

81
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:27,000
Attack three: SIM swapping.

82
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,000
An attacker transfers your phone number.

83
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Now they receive your SMS two-factor codes.

84
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Use an authenticator app — not SMS —

85
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,500
for all exchange accounts.

86
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:41,500
Attack four: fake support scams.

87
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:44,500
Someone pretends to be support staff.

88
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:47,500
They ask for your seed phrase.

89
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:51,000
No legitimate protocol ever asks

90
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,500
for your seed phrase. Ever.

91
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:56,500
Attack five: clipboard hijacking malware.

92
00:03:56,500 --> 00:03:59,500
Malware replaces your copied address

93
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:01,500
with the attacker's address.

94
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:04,500
Always verify the first four

95
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:07,000
and last four characters before confirming.

96
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,500
Here is the actionable checklist.

97
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:12,500
One: Move significant funds off exchanges.

98
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:15,500
Two: Buy hardware from the official website.

99
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:18,500
Three: generate seed phrase on the device.

100
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:21,500
Four: Write your seed phrase on metal.

101
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:24,500
Five: Never share your seed phrase.

102
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:27,500
Six: Use an authenticator app for 2FA.

103
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:30,500
Seven: Verify destination addresses.

104
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:33,500
Eight: Use a dedicated device for large sends.

105
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:36,500
Nine: Bookmark wallet URLs — never search.

106
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:39,500
Ten: Revoke unused DeFi approvals quarterly.

107
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,500
Security is not a product you buy.

108
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:45,000
It is a practice you build.

109
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
The most valuable thing in crypto

110
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
is not the asset.

111
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,500
It is the practice of keeping it.

112
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:55,500
Anyone can buy cryptocurrency.

113
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:58,500
Fewer people know how to hold it safely.

114
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:01,500
That knowledge is Chain Clarity Phase 4 —

115
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:04,000
and you now have it.

116
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Phase 5: where blockchain goes

117
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,500
beyond finance entirely.

118
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:11,500
Subscribe. We will see you there.
